  "question": {
    "text": "Consider a binary star system of star <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and star <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> with masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub></math> revolving in a circular orbit of radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;respectively. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub></math> are the time period of star <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and star <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo></math> respectively, then:",
    "image": null
  },
